<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Rousseau is a 54-year-old man with delusional disorder, persecutory type, seven weeks into aripiprazole 5 mg each morning, and eleven weeks after a bulbar urethroplasty. Two days ago, after an afternoon splitting firewood, he felt a pulling pain across the lower back that is worse when he bends or rises from a chair. It does not travel down either leg. He has no numbness in the groin or buttocks, no weakness, no fever, no weight loss, and no history of cancer. He is passing urine with a good stream and has had no difficulty starting or emptying, which worried him most, because difficulty passing urine was his life for decades. He has taken acetaminophen twice a day with some relief. He asked whether the pain could have been caused by the operation or by his tablets; it was explained that neither is likely. He has had no restlessness on aripiprazole and is sleeping about six hours. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, after splitting firewood</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Lower back, both sides, no radiation</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days, slowly easing</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Pulling, worse with bending and rising</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Six out of ten at worst; walking slowly</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Acetaminophen and lying on his side help</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No leg pain, numbness, weakness, fever, or change in passing urine or stool</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

